  "home_tagline": "Professor of Economics · University of Exeter",

  "home_text": "I study how people make decisions — and how better decisions can improve lives. My research combines field experiments and randomised controlled trials with insights from behavioural economics, spanning global health, poverty, crime, financial behaviour, and wellbeing. I work with governments, NGOs, and international organisations to design and evaluate interventions that work in the real world.",

  "bio": [
    "I am a Professor of Economics at the University of Exeter, working at the intersection of behavioural economics, global health, and development economics. My research uses field experiments and randomised controlled trials to understand how people make decisions — and how those decisions can be improved through well-designed policy.",
    "My work spans cognitive performance and poverty, preference elicitation, wellbeing measurement, and the behavioural underpinnings of health outcomes. I have collaborated with governments, NGOs, and international organisations across Africa, Asia, and the Americas.",
    "Before joining Exeter, I held positions at the World Bank, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, and the Federal Reserve Bank of Boston, as well as academic affiliations with Yale, MIT, and Harvard."
  ],

