  "research_intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "My research sits at the intersection of behavioural economics, global health, and development. The central question running through my work is: what prevents people from making decisions that are in their own best interests, and what can be done about it? I use randomised controlled trials and field experiments — often in low- and middle-income countries — to study how cognitive, psychological, and material constraints shape economic behaviour and wellbeing.",
      "A recurring theme is the relationship between poverty and decision-making: not only how material deprivation affects choices, but how interventions targeting behaviour can complement or amplify economic support. I also work on methodological questions in causal inference and preference measurement that arise in this kind of applied work."
    ],
    "tags_note": "Papers are tagged by their primary research area. Many span multiple themes — use the filters to explore by topic. 'Global Health & Development' covers field experiments on health, poverty alleviation, and development interventions. 'Behavioural & Experimental' covers lab and field experiments on decision-making. 'Methodology' covers papers primarily about research methods. 'Microeconomic Theory' covers game theory, auctions, and mathematical economics. 'Wellbeing, Welfare & Ethics' covers normative and philosophical work."
  },

